#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100876 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100697 :question/id #uuid "9832238f-baff-5527-a11a-c65b6529cb0b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4410" :question/problem "Calculate the product $C D,$ if\n\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n\nand \n\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $D$ is the $2$nd *column* of the identity matrix $I_3.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $3\\times 3$ matrix $C$ by the $2$nd *column* of the identity matrix $I_3,$ the result must be the $2$nd *column* of $C.$ Therefore,\n\n\n\n$$\nCD=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n\n\nDon't believe it? Let's check the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CD & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 0 \\\\ -1\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 5\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89362140-1a17-56fd-a9ab-4da336112bee" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050825 :topic/difficulty 0.48023065847230173 :topic/id #uuid "69b57f07-ad86-5963-8f81-f63cb01f239f" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100868}] :topic/math-academy-id 1195 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050463}] :topic/title "Multiplying a Matrix by a Column Vector"}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725533 :question-genera